      "math_academy_id": "q-50858",
      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-3)^{n-1}}{n}{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor series expansion about $x=0$ of ${f}^{′}(x).$",
      "worked_solution": "Writing the first few terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-3)^{n-1}}{n}{x}^{n}=x-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+3{x}^{3}-⋯.\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}^{′}(x),$ we differentiate term-by-term. This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=1-3x+9{x}^{2}-⋯.\n$$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
